\subsubsection{Solutions and a Key Assumption}
 The state trajectories of the  system formed by connecting (PDM), (FOPM) and (EDIM) in feedback (see~Figure~\ref{fig:closedloop}) is $(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{s},\mathbf{x})$, which is a Carath\'{e}odory solution for a given $x(0)$ in $\mathbb{X}$. Notice that, according to~\cite[Proposition~S2]{Cortes_2008aa}, such a solution is guaranteed to exist because $\mathfrak{V}$, $\mathcal{G}_\mathfrak{F}$, and $\mathcal{H}_\mathfrak{F}$ are locally bounded, and $\mathfrak{V}$ takes values in a non-empty, compact, and convex set.

We observe that Rademacher's Theorem implies that all $\mathbf{x}$ in $\mathscr{X}$ and $\mathbf{p}$ in $\mathscr{P}$ are differentiable for almost all time $t \geq 0$. We will be implicitly using this fact when writing integrals with respect to time of integrands involving functions of $\dot{\mathbf{x}}$ and $\dot{\mathbf{p}}$. For this reason, henceforth, all integrals are in the sense of Lebesgue. \\

\begin{assumption}
\label{ass:PayoffMech} We assume that any payoff mechanism $\mathfrak{P}$ considered henceforth satisfies the following conditions.
\begin{enumerate}%[label=(\roman*)]
    \item There is a Lipschitz continuous map $\mathcal{F}_\mathfrak{P}:\mathbb{X} \to \mathbb{R}^n$ such that for every $\mathbf{x}$ in $\mathscr{X}$, the following holds:
    $$ \lim_{t \rightarrow \infty} \| \dot{x}(t) \|=0 \implies \lim_{t \rightarrow \infty} \big \| p(t)-\mathcal{F}_{\mathfrak{P}} \big(x(t)\big) \big \| = 0.$$ We refer to $\mathcal{F}_{\mathfrak{P}}$ as the \uline{stationary game} of $\mathfrak{P}$.
    \item For the map $\mathfrak{F}:\mathbf{x} \mapsto \mathbf{u}$ specified by (PDM) there is $\beta_{\mathfrak{F}} > 0$ such that
    $$ \sup_{t \geq 0} \| u(t) \|_\infty \leq \beta_{\mathfrak{F}}, \ \mathfrak{F}:\mathbf{x} \mapsto \mathbf{u}, \ \mathbf{x} \in \mathscr{X}.$$
\end{enumerate}
\end{assumption}
% \vspace{.1 in}

Assumption~\ref{ass:PayoffMech}.1 is akin to the existence of an input-to-state characteristic~\cite{Angeli2006Systems-With-Co}. Since $\|\mathbf{x}\|_{\infty} \leq 1$, Assumption~\ref{ass:PayoffMech}.2 is satisfied for any bounded-input bounded-output (BIBO) stable $\mathfrak{F}$. Notice that because (FOPM) is also BIBO stable, Assumption~\ref{ass:PayoffMech}.2 implies that there is $\beta_{\mathfrak{P}} > 0$ such that $ \| p(t) \|_\infty <  \beta_{\mathfrak{P}}$ for all $t \geq 0$.

\subsubsection{Potential and Contractive Games} We call $\mathfrak{F}$ a {\it game} when for all $t\geq 0$ it acts as a memoryless map ${\mathcal{F}:x(t) \mapsto p(t)}$, where $\mathcal{F}:\mathbb{X} \to \mathbb{R}^n$ is Lipschitz continuous. Here, stability and the static gain of (FOPM) imply that $ \mathcal{F}_\mathfrak{P}= \gamma(\nu+\mu^{-1})\mathcal{F}$.
\begin{definition} \label{def:game}  We refer to a game $\mathcal{F}$ as a \uline{potential game} when there is a potential $f:\mathbb{X} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying\footnote{See~\cite{Sandholm2001Potential-games} and references therein for a detailed discussion.}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:PotentialGame}
f\big ( x(T) \big) - f\big ( x(0) \big) = \int_0^T \dot{x}'(t)\mathcal{F}\big ( x(t) \big) dt, \ \mathbf{x} \in \mathscr{X}.
\end{equation}
\end{definition} 
% \vspace{.1 in}

 Hence, $\mathcal{F}(x)=Ax+b$ is a potential game for any $b$ in $\mathbb{R}^n$ and $A=A'$ in $\mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$, with $f(x) = \tfrac{1}{2} x'Ax + b'x$. 

\begin{definition} \label{def:contr}
    We refer to a game $\mathcal{F}$ as \uline{contractive} when
    $$ (y-x)'(\mathcal{F}(y)-\mathcal{F}(x))\le 0,\quad x,y\in\mathbb{X}. $$
\end{definition}
% \vspace{.1 in}

Any potential game with a concave potential function is a contractive game \cite{Hofbauer2007Stable-games}. The congestion game in~\cite[Example~13.2]{Sandholm2015Handbook-of-gam} is a well-known example of a nonlinear contractive potential game. There are also many examples of contractive games that are not potential games such as matching in symmetric zero-sum games \cite[Chapter 3.3]{Sandholm2010Population-Game}.

\subsubsection{Linear Time Invariant Payoff Mechanism}
The payoff mechanism could be linear time invariant, as defined below.

\begin{definition}{\bf [LTI]}\label{def:LTIPM} A payoff mechanism $\mathfrak{P}$ is of the linear time invariant (LTI) type if $\mathfrak{F}$, specified by (PDM), has a proper rational $n \times n$ transfer function matrix $F(s)$ whose poles have negative real part so as to satisfy Assumption~\ref{ass:PayoffMech}.  The associated stationary game is $\mathcal{F}_\mathfrak{P}(x)=\gamma(\nu+\mu^{-1})F(0)x$.
\end{definition} 

% \vspace{.1in}

\subsubsection{Nash Equilibria Set And Best Response Map} Using the map $\mathcal{F}_\mathfrak{P}$ specified in Assumption~\ref{ass:PayoffMech} for a given $\mathfrak{P}$, we can define the associated Nash equilibrium set as follows.

\begin{definition}[\bf Nash equilibria]
Given a payoff mechanism $\mathfrak{P}$, we define the Nash equilibria set\cite{Jr.1951Non-Cooperative} of $\mathcal{F}_\mathfrak{P}$ as:
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{NE}(\mathcal{F}_\mathfrak{P}) : = \Big \{ x \in \mathbb{X} \ \big | \ x \in \mathfrak{B} \big (\mathcal{F}_\mathfrak{P}(x) \big ) \Big \},
\end{equation*}
where $\mathfrak{B}:\mathbb{R}^n \to 2^\mathbb{X}$ is the best response map defined in (\ref{eq:bestresponsemap}).
\end{definition}

Thus, $\mathbb{NE}(\mathcal{F}_\mathfrak{F})$ are the fixed points of $\mathfrak{B} \circ \mathcal{F}_\mathfrak{P}$. If $x^* \in \mathbb{NE}(\mathcal{F}_\mathfrak{P})$ with $p = \mathcal{F}_\mathfrak{P}(x^*)$, the population has no incentive to deviate. 

\begin{remark}[\bf (NS) and optimization]\label{rem:NSExplanation} 
Condition (NS) is termed \emph{Nash stationarity} because, for a game  \( p(t)=\mathcal{F}(x(t)) \), it ensures the (EDM) reaches equilibrium precisely when \( x \in \mathbb{NE}(\mathcal{F}) \). Importantly for optimization, if \( \mathcal{F} \) is a potential game with concave potential \( f \), the set \( \mathbb{NE}(\mathcal{F}) \) corresponds to the maxima of \( f \) in \( \mathbb{X} \) (see~\cite[\S3,\S5]{Sandholm2001Potential-games}).
\end{remark}



\section{System-theoretic Passivity Concepts}
\label{sec:MainResult}

We will later employ in \S\ref{sec:main_results} two nonstandard notions of system-theoretic passivity to $\mathfrak{F}$, as specified by (PDM), to establish convergence properties for $\mathbf{x}$. In this section, we will define these notions and explore their basic properties.

\subsection{Counterclockwise dissipativity (CCW)}
We start by adapting to our framework the concept of counterclockwise systems proposed in~\cite{Angeli2006Systems-With-Co} to study stability and robustness of nonlinear feedback systems. \\

\begin{definition}{\bf [CCW]} The map $\mathfrak{F}:\mathbf{x} \mapsto \mathbf{u}$ is counterclockwise dissipative (or CCW for short) when
\begin{equation} \label{eq:CCWDEf} \inf_{T>0,\mathbf{x} \in \mathscr{X}} \int_0^T \dot{u}'(t)x(t)dt > -\infty.
\end{equation}
\end{definition} 
% \vspace{.1 in}

\subsubsection{Examples of $\mathfrak{F}$ satisfying CCW}

Interestingly, when $\mathfrak{F}$ is a game $\mathcal{F}$, we know from \cite[Theorem~1]{Martins_2025aa} that $\mathfrak{F}$ is CCW if and only if $\mathcal{F}$ is potential. Modifying the proof of \cite[Theorem~1]{Angeli2006Systems-With-Co} one can show that an LTI payoff mechanism (Definition~\ref{def:LTIPM}) is CCW if the negative imaginary (NI) condition $j(F(j\omega)-F'(-j\omega))\succeq 0$ holds for all $\omega>0$. The concept of (NI) for multiple inputs and outputs was proposed in~\cite{Lanzon2008Stability-Robus} to study stability and robustness. Relevant subclasses of (NI) systems and a dissipative framework for stability are studied in~\cite{Lanzon2023Characterizatio}. 
\begin{remark} \label{rem_conepayoffs} If $\mathfrak{F}$ and $\mathfrak{G}$ are CCW then $\xi \mathfrak{F}+\zeta \mathfrak{G}$ is also CCW for any non-negative $\xi$ and $\zeta$, implying that the set of CCW maps is a convex cone (see also \cite[Proposition~III.1]{Angeli2006Systems-With-Co}). As a result, the already large class of (NI) systems~\cite{Petersen2016Negative-imagin} and nonlinear generalizations~\cite{Ghallab2018Extending-Negat} that are CCW can be combined with potential games (see \cite[Theorem~2]{Martins_2025aa}) to form a broad convex cone of CCW maps. 
\end{remark}

The following is an example interpretable as a potential game with an LTI additive perturbation we denote as $\mathfrak{G}$. 

\begin{example}\label{ex_NIAndPotential} For a potential game $\mathcal{F}$, a given time-constant $\lambda^{-1}>0$, $b$ in $\mathbb{R}^n$, and $A=A'$ in $\mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$, consider
\begin{subequations}
\label{eq_examplepayoff}
\begin{align}
\dot{q}(t) &= \lambda \big ( Ax(t) + b - q(t) \big ), \qquad t\geq0, \quad q(0)=0, \\
u(t) &= \mathcal{F}\big ( x(t) \big ) + k \lambda \big ( A x(t) + b - q(t) \big)
\end{align}
\end{subequations} where $k$ is a real constant satisfying $k A \preceq 0$.
\end{example}


The transfer function matrix $G(s)$ from $\mathbf{x}$ to $k \lambda ( A \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{q})$ is NI satisfying ${j(G(j\omega)-G'(-j\omega)) = -2 k \lambda^2 \tfrac{\omega}{\omega^2+\lambda^2}A}$, and we conclude from Remark~\ref{rem_conepayoffs} that~(\ref{eq_examplepayoff}) is CCW with stationary game $\mathcal{F}(x)$. Notice that the effect of $b$ in~(\ref{eq_examplepayoff}) vanishes exponentially fast and plays no role in testing for CCW. 

\begin{remark}\label{rem:detailsaboutexampls} Example~\ref{ex_NIAndPotential} generalizes well-motivated examples in~\cite{Fox2013Population-Game}. Namely,\cite[(83)]{Fox2013Population-Game} follows by specializing~(\ref{eq_examplepayoff}) with $k=1$ and $\mathcal{F} ( x )=Ax+b$, where $A \prec 0$. The example \cite[(77)]{Fox2013Population-Game} follows by specializing~(\ref{eq_examplepayoff}) with $k=-\lambda^{-2}$, and $\mathcal{F} ( x )=\lambda^{-1}(Ax+b)$. With this parameter choice, we must impose $A \succeq 0$, such as in a coordination game, in contrast with~\cite[(77)]{Fox2013Population-Game} where $A \prec 0$.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Definition and properties of $\delta$-passivity} \label{subsec:dpass}

The notion of $\delta$-passivity was developed within the context of population games in order to study and generalize contractive games \cite{Fox2013Population-Game}.

\begin{definition} \label{def:dpass}
    {\bf [$\delta$-passive]} A given EDIM is qualified as $\delta$-passive when for any given $\mathbf{p}$ in $\mathscr{P}$ and any solution $\mathbf{x}$ in $\mathscr{X}$ for (EDIM) it holds that
    $$ \inf_{T>0}\int_0^T \dot{p}'(t)\dot{x}(t)dt>-\infty. $$

    Similarly, a payoff mechanism map $\mathfrak{F}:\mathbf{x}\mapsto\mathbf{u}$ is qualified as $\delta$-antipassive when
    $$ \sup_{T>0,\mathbf{x}\in\mathscr{X}}\int_0^T \dot{u}'(t)\dot{x}(t)dt < \infty.$$
\end{definition}
% \vspace{.1 in}

\begin{remark}
From~\cite[Theorem~2]{Certorio_2024aa}, the EDIM is $\delta$-passive if either $\beta=0$ or $\mathcal{T}$ can be expressed as $\mathcal{T}=\alpha^\mathrm{SEPT}\mathcal{T}^\mathrm{SEPT}+\alpha^\mathrm{ND}\mathcal{T}^\mathrm{ND}$ for some (SEPT) rule $\mathcal{T}^\mathrm{SEPT}$, (ND-IPC) rule $\mathcal{T}^\mathrm{ND}$, and nonnegative weights $\alpha^\mathrm{SEPT}$ and $\alpha^\mathrm{ND}$. Examples of rules leading to $\delta$-passive EDIMs include $\mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{\tiny Smith}}$, $\mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{\tiny BNN}}$ and $\mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{\tiny (1)}}$ given in Examples~\ref{example:Smith}, \ref{ex:BNN} and \ref{ex:hybrid}, respectively.
\end{remark}

All contractive games for which $\mathcal{F}$ is continuously differentiable are $\delta$-antipassive \cite{Fox2013Population-Game}. The ``smoothing-anticipatory" PDMs from \cite{Park2019From-Population} provide dynamic examples of $\delta$-antipassive payoff mechanisms beyond contractive games.

\begin{remark}
    The set of $\delta$-antipassive maps is a convex cone.
\end{remark}

\section{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

The following is a key lemma.

\begin{lemma} \label{lemma} Consider that a $\delta$-passive (EDIM) with well-behaved rule $\mathcal{T}$ and a payoff mechanism satisfying Assumption~\ref{ass:PayoffMech} are given, and that $\mathbf{x}$ in $\mathscr{X}$ is a solution of (EDIM) with $\mathfrak{P}:\mathbf{x} \mapsto \mathbf{p}$. If the following inequality is satisfied
\begin{equation} \label{eq:intbounded}
\int_0^\infty \rho \big( x(t),p(t) \big) dt < \infty,
\end{equation} indicating that the integral is bounded, then it holds that
\begin{equation} \label{eq:dto0}
\lim_{t \rightarrow \infty} d \Big( x(t), \mathfrak{B} \big ( p(t) \big) \Big) = 0,
\end{equation} where $d \big ( x, \mathbb{S} \big) : = \inf_{y \in \mathbb{S}} \| x-y \|$ for $\mathbb{S} \subset \mathbb{X}$. If, in addition to the conditions of the lemma, there is no best response component in (EDIM), that is $\alpha=0$,  then it holds that
\begin{equation} \label{eq:dtoNE}
\lim_{t \rightarrow \infty} d \Big( x(t), \mathbb{NE} \big ( \mathcal{F}_{\mathfrak{P}} \big) \Big) = 0.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

% \vspace{.1in}

\textbf{Proof:} By Proposition~\ref{prop:rho} along with the Lipschitz continuity of $(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{p})$ with respect to time, $\rho(x(t),p(t))$ is Lipschitz continuous along trajectories. With (\ref{eq:intbounded}), {B}arb\u{a}lat's Lemma~\cite{Farkas2016Variations-on-B} ensures $\lim_{t\to\infty}\rho(x(t),p(t))=0$. Via the limit and continuity of $\rho$, all accumulation points must be zeros of the correlation function. With Assumption~\ref{ass:PayoffMech}.2, the existence of accumulation points is guaranteed as the trajectory is bounded. If (\ref{eq:dto0}) did not hold, there would exist an accumulation point $(x^\star,p^\star)$ of $(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{p})$ such that $d(x^\star,\mathfrak{B}(p^\star))>0$, which would imply $x^\star\notin\mathfrak{B}(p^\star)$, and $\rho(x^\star,p^\star)=0$. However, by Proposition~\ref{prop:rho}, this would present a contradiction as $\rho(x^\star,p^\star)=0$ would imply $x^\star\in\mathfrak{B}(p^\star)$. Thus, we conclude that (\ref{eq:dto0}) must hold.

Without a best response component, (\ref{eq:dtoNE}) is guaranteed via the proof of \cite[Theorem~2]{Martins_2025aa}, in which a bound identical to (\ref{eq:intbounded}) is used to prove convergence to the Nash equilibria set for well-behaved continuous learning rules.$\square$

When the conditions of Lemma~\ref{lemma} are met, (\ref{eq:dto0}) requires that $x(t)$ tends to $\mathfrak{B}(p(t))$. When $\alpha=0$, the result is strengthened to (\ref{eq:dtoNE}). Knowing that $x(t)$ will converge to the Nash equilibrium set of the stationary game can greatly simplify the design of a payoff mechanism as it is only necessary to ensure the placement of the Nash equilibria.

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:1}
Consider a $\delta$-passive (EDIM) with well-behaved rule $\mathcal{T}$ and a payoff mechanism $\mathfrak{P}$ satisfying Assumption \ref{ass:PayoffMech} for which $\mathfrak{F}$ is CCW and $\mathfrak{P}$ satisfies one of the following $\delta$-passivity conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\mathfrak{F}$ is $\delta$-antipassive and $\gamma\nu>0$,
    \item $\mathfrak{F}$ is $\delta$-passive and $\gamma\nu<0$, or
    \item $\gamma\nu=0$.
\end{enumerate}
For any solution $\mathbf{x}$ of (EDIM) with ${\mathfrak{P}:\mathbf{x} \mapsto \mathbf{p}}$, % obtained via $\mathfrak{P}:\mathbf{x}\mapsto\mathbf{p}$,
\begin{equation}
    \int_0^\infty \rho \big( x(t),p(t) \big) dt < \infty,
\end{equation}
guaranteeing (\ref{eq:dto0}) and---if there is no best response component in (EDIM)---(\ref{eq:dtoNE}) will hold.
\end{theorem}

% \vspace{.1in}

\textbf{Proof:} There exist constants $c_1,c_2,c_3\in\mathbb{R}$ such that
\begin{equation}\arraycolsep=1.4pt
\left.
\begin{array}{rl} \label{eq:ineq}
    \int_0^T \dot{p}'(t)\dot{x}(t)dt &\ge c_1 \\[1pt]
    -\mu\gamma(\nu+\mu^{-1})\int_0^T \dot{u}'(t)x(t)dt &\le c_2 \\[1pt]
    \gamma\nu\int_0^T \dot{u}'(t)\dot{x}(t)dt &\le c_3
\end{array} \right\}
\end{equation}
holds for any $T>0$ as (EDIM) is $\delta$-passive, $\mathfrak{F}$ is CCW, and $\mathfrak{P}$ satisfies one of the $\delta$-passivity conditions from the theorem statement.

From Assumption \ref{ass:PayoffMech}.2, integration by parts, and (\ref{eq:ineq}),
\begin{align*}
    c_1 &\le \int_0^T \dot{p}'(t)\dot{x}(t)= \gamma\int_0^T (\dot{s}(t)+\nu\dot{u}(t))'\dot{x}(t)dt  \\
        &\le \gamma\int_0^T (u(t)-\mu s(t))'\dot{x}(t)dt + c_3 \\
        &= -\mu \int_0^T p'(t)\dot{x}(t)dt + \gamma(1+\mu\nu)\int_0^T u'(t)\dot{x}(t) + c_3 \\
        &\le -\mu\int_0^T\rho(x(t),p(t))dt + c_2 + 2 \gamma(1+\mu\nu)\beta_\mathfrak{F} + c_3,
\end{align*}
which we rearrange for
\begin{equation*}
    \int_0^T \rho(x(t),p(t))dt \le 2\gamma(\nu+\mu^{-1})\beta_\mathfrak{F} + \frac{1}{\mu}(c_2 + c_3 - c_1).
\end{equation*}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:rho}, $\rho(x(t),p(t))\ge0$, yielding
\begin{equation*}
    \int_0^\infty \rho(x(t),p(t))dt \le 2\gamma(\nu+\mu^{-1})\beta_\mathfrak{F} + \frac{1}{\mu}(c_2 + c_3 - c_1).
\end{equation*}
With a finite upper-bound established, the desired convergence results follow immediately from Lemma~\ref{lemma}. $\square$

\begin{corollary} \label{cor:1}
    With slight modification to the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:1}, the convergence results can be extended to payoff mechanisms, $\mathfrak{H}:\mathscr{X}\to\mathscr{P}$, expressible as $\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{P}+\mathfrak{G}$, where $\mathfrak{P}$ meets the requirements of Theorem~\ref{thm:1}, including a first order payoff modification, and (i) $\mathfrak{G}$ is CCW, $\delta$-antipassive, and satisfies Assumption~\ref{ass:PayoffMech}, or (ii) $\mathfrak{G}$ satisfies the conditions of Theorem~\ref{thm:1} and both $\mathfrak{P}$ and $\mathfrak{G}$ use the same value for $\mu$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Numerical Example}

To validate our main results, we consider a Braess' paradox inspired congestion game with the addition of a dynamic toll, shown in Figure~\ref{fig:pdm_congestion}. We consider three strategies representing distinct routes from A to D: (1) ABD, (2) ACBD, and (3) ACD. Aside from the toll, the costs along each edge represent traversal times. This induces the following payoffs:
\begin{equation*}
    p=\mathcal{F}(x) - \begin{bmatrix}
        0 \\
        b\tau_\mathrm{toll} \\
        0
    \end{bmatrix}, \mathcal{F}(x):=-\begin{bmatrix}
        7 + 2(x_1+x_2) \\
        5 + 5x_2 + 2(x_1+x_3) \\
        7 + 2(x_2+x_3)
    \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation*}

Without the toll, the Nash equilibrium of the congestion game is $(0.25,0.5,0.25)'$, which results in an average payoff of $-8.5$. However, the average payoff can be improved by implementing a toll to drive the equilibrium to $(0.5,0,0.5)'$, which results in an average payoff of $-8$.

All congestion games are potential and thus CCW. If each edge cost is a non-decreasing function of the traffic across the edge, the congestion game is also $\delta$-antipassive. Referencing Corollary~\ref{cor:1}, $\mathfrak{G}$ is the congestion game absent the toll, and $\mathfrak{P}$ uses $u(t)=-(0,x_2(t),0)$, $\nu=0$, $\gamma=b$---using the (FOPM) to implement a toll for which $\dot{\tau}_\mathrm{toll}(t)=x_2(t)-\mu\tau_\mathrm{toll}(t)$---resulting in the stationary game,
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{F}_\mathrm{s}(x) = - \begin{bmatrix}
        7 + 2(x_1+x_2) \\
        5 + (5+b\mu^{-1})x_2 + 2(x_1+x_3) \\
        7 +2(x_2 + x_3).
    \end{bmatrix},
\end{equation*}
with Nash equilibrium $(\tfrac{1}{2}(1+b\mu^{-1}),1,\tfrac{1}{2}(1+b\mu^{-1}))'/(2+b\mu^{-1})$. If $\mu\ll b$, the Nash equilibrium will be close to $(0.5,0,0.5)$, meaning precise knowledge of the value of $b$---a transfer constant which models the mapping from tolls to ``equivalent" traversal times---is unnecessary.


The simulation \cite{hankins_nash_2025} uses $b=1$, $\mu=100^{-1}$, and the learning rules from examples \ref{example:Smith}, \ref{ex:BNN}, and \ref{ex:hybrid} with distinct initial conditions: $x^\mathrm{Smith}(0) = (0,0.9,0.1)'$, $x^\mathrm{BNN}(0) = (0,0,1)'$, and $x^\mathrm{(1)}(0) = (0.6, 0.3, 0.1)'$. Each learning rule-initial condition pair is simulated with $\alpha=1$ and $\alpha=0$. We set $\beta$ to $\tfrac{1}{10}$ for the simulation of Example \ref{ex:hybrid} and $\beta=1$ otherwise. The results are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:plot} and demonstrate the convergence guaranteed by Corollary~\ref{cor:1}.


