For the successor computation $(Z, \Inact) \xra{a, (C_1, \dots,
      C_m)} (Z', \Inact')$, we have $\llbracket (Z',
    \Inact') \rrbracket = \{ \gamma' \mid \exists \gamma \in
    \llbracket (Z, \Inact) \rrbracket, \exists d \ge 0 \text{ s.t. }
    \gamma \xra{d, a, (C_1, \dots, C_m)} \gamma' \}$.
  \end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    Let $S = \{\gamma' \mid \exists \gamma \in (Z,\Inact), \exists d \geq 0 \text{ s.t. }\gamma \xra{d,a,(C_{1},\dots,C_{n})} \gamma'\}$. To prove $S \subseteq (Z',\Inact')$, we take some $\gamma' \in S$ where for some $\gamma \in (Z,\Inact)$, $\gamma \xra{d,a,(C_{1},\dots,C_{n})} \gamma'$. Let $\gamma_{1} = \{(q_{1},v_{1}),\dots,(q_{n},v_{n})\}$ such that $\gamma \xra{d} \gamma_{1}$. Firstly, we see that by successor computation of $(Z,\Inact)$, for the intermediate zone $(Z_{1},\Inact_{1})$, $\gamma_{1} \in (Z_{1},\Inact_{1})$. Also, by definition this means $\gamma' = \bigcup_{i=1}^{n} M_{i}$ where $M_{i}$ is some minimal model of $C_{i}$ on $v_{i}$. To show $\gamma' \in (Z',\Inact')$, we look at each variable of $(Z',\Inact)$ and define a mapping for that variable to some state in $\gamma'$ and show that this mapping implies $\gamma' \in (Z',\Inact)$. We look at all the possible cases when variables were added for $(Z',\Inact')$ during successor comuptation:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item For variables $x_{q,i} \in Var(Z')$ added because of some $q$ conjunct in some $C_{j} \in C$, the constraints on $x_{q,i}$ will be the constraints of $x_{q_{j},i_{j}}$ in $(Z_{1},\Inact_{1})$ along with the constraints of $I_{j}$ for disjuncts $I_{j}$ in $C_{j}$ if any. Now, as $M_{j} \subseteq \gamma'$ for a minimal model $M_{j}$ of $C_{j}$ on $v_{j}$, it means if some $I_{j}$ is a conjunct of $C_{j}$, then $v_{j} \in I_{j}$. Also, there will be a $(q,v_{j}) \in \gamma'$ because of the conjunct $q$ in $C_{j}$. We will map $x_{q,i}$ to that $(q,v_{j})$.

      \item For variable $x_{q,0} \in \Inact'$ added because of some $q$ conjunct in some $C_{j} \in C$, by the construction of $(Z',\Inact')$ the variable $x_{q_{j},i_{j}} \in Var(Z_{1},\Inact_{1})$ is in fact a variable in $\Inact_{1}$. This means because $\gamma_{1} \in (Z_{1},\Inact_{1})$, $val((q_{j},v_{j})) = \bot$, or $v_{j} = \bot$. Also, as there is some $M_{j} \subseteq \gamma'$ where $M_{j}$ is a minimal model of $C_{j}$ on $v_{j}$, $(q,v_{j}) \in \gamma'$, or $(q,\bot) \in \gamma'$. We map $x_{q,0}$ to this $(q,\bot)$.

      \item For variables $x_{q,i} \in Var(Z')$ added because of some $x.q$ comjunct in some $C_{j} \in C$, the constraints on $x_{q,i}$ in the zone will be $x_{q,i} = 0$. Now, As there is some $M_{j} \subseteq \gamma'$ where $M_{j}$ is a minimal model of $C_{j}$ on $v_{j}$, it means there will be a state $(q,0) \in M_{j}$ i.e. $(q,0) \in \gamma'$ for that particular state $q$. We will map $x_{q,i}$ to that $(q,0)$.

      \item For variable $x_{q,0} \in \Inact'$ added because of some $\inact{x}.q$ conjunct in $C_{j} \in C$, as there is some $M_{j} \subseteq \gamma'$ for a minimal model $M_{j}$ of $C_{j}$ on $v_{j}$, it means $(q,\bot) \in M_{j}$ and so $(q,\bot) \in \gamma'$. We map $x_{q,0}$ to this $(q,\bot)$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Thus we have a mapping from $Var(Z',\Inact')$ to $\gamma'$ such that all the constraints of $Z'$ are satisfied and the variables of $\Inact'$ are mapped to states with values $\bot$, so $\gamma' \in (Z',\Inact')$.

    Now, to prove the other direction, i.e. that $(Z',\Inact') \subseteq S$, we look at some $\gamma'$ such that $\gamma' \in (Z',\Inact')$ by mapping $h$. To prove that $\gamma' \in S$, i.e that there is some $\gamma \in (Z,\Inact)$ and $d \geq 0$ such that $\gamma \xra{d,a,(C_{1},\dots,C_{n})} \gamma'$, it is enough to prove that there is some $\gamma_{1} \in (Z_{1},\Inact_{1})$ such that $\gamma_{1} \xra{a,(C_{1},\dots,C_{n})}$ because by construction, any $\gamma_{1} \in (Z_{1},\Inact_{1})$ only when there is some $\gamma \in (Z,\Inact)$ and $t \geq 0$ such that $\gamma + t = \gamma_{1}$. To do so, we construct the required $\gamma_{1}$ such that the following hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item If $Var(Z,\Inact) = \{x_{q_{1},i_{1}},\dots,x_{q_{n},i_{i}}\}$, we say $\gamma_{1} = \{(q_{1},v_{1}),\dots,(q_{n},v_{n})\}$
      \item If there is a variable $x_{q,i} \in Var(Z')$ that was added because of a $q$ conjunct in some $C_{j}$, we set $v_{j}$ to $val(h'(x_{q,i}))$. 
      \item If there is a variable $x_{q,i} \in \Inact'$ that was added because of conjunct $q$ in $C_{j}$, we set $v_{j} = \bot$. 
      \item If there is some variable $x_{q,i}  \in Var(Z',\Inact')$ that was added because of a $x.q$ or a $\inact{x}.q$ conjunct in some $C_{j}$, we set the value of $v_{j}$ to be an arbitrary real such that it satisfies the constraints of $Z_{1}$ and $v_{j} \in I_{j}$ for any $I_{j}$ conjunct present in $C_{j}$. (Such a value will exist because otherwise, in the construction of $(Z',\Inact')$, the zone $Z_{2}$ would have been empty.)
    \end{enumerate} 
    Now, we see that because $\gamma_{1}$ will satisfy all constraints of $(Z_{1},\Inact_{1})$ by mapping $m$ with $m(x_{q_{k},i_{j}}) = (q_{k},v_{k})$, $\gamma_{1} \in (Z_{1},\Inact_{1})$. Also by our definition of $\gamma_{1}$, we see that $\gamma_{1} \xra{a,(C_{1},\dots,C_{n})} \gamma'$. 
  \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:zonecas}
    The zone graph is sound and complete.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We need to prove that for every path $Z_{0} \xra{a_{1},C^1} Z_{1} \dots Z_{n-1} \xra{a_{n},C^n} Z_{n}$ in the zone graph, there exists a path in the configuration graph of the form $\gamma_{0} \xra{t_{1},a_{1},C^{'1}} \dots \xra{t_{n},a_{n},C^{'n}} \gamma_{n}$ such that $\gamma_{i} \in Z_{i}$ for every $0 \leq i \leq n$, and vice versa. To prove the latter direction, we see that firstly, $\gamma_{0} \in Z_{0}$ by definition. Also, by Lemma \ref{lem:succ-zone}, if $\gamma \in Z$ and $\gamma \xra{d,a,C} \gamma'$ for some $d \ge 0$, then for the zone $Z \xra{a,C} Z'$, $\gamma' \in Z'$. This means that for some path $\gamma_{0} \xra{t_{1},a_{1},C^{'1}}\gamma_{1}\dots\gamma_{n-1}\xra{t_{n},a_{n},C^{'n}} \gamma_{n}$ of the configuration graph, we can always get the path $Z_{0} \xra{a_{1},C^1} Z_{1} \dots Z_{n-1} \xra{a_{n},C^n} Z_{n}$ in the zone graph with $\gamma_{i} \in Z_{i}$ for each $0 \leq i \leq n$. Now to prove the soundness direction, looking at a path $Z_{0} \xra{a_{1},C^1} Z_{1} \dots Z_{n-1} \xra{a_{n},C^n} Z_{n}$ of the zone graph, because each of these zones are non-empty and using Lemma \ref{lem:succ-zone}, we can see that a path $\gamma_{0} \xra{t_{1},a_{1},C^{'1}}\gamma_{1}\dots\gamma_{n-1}\xra{t_{n},a_{n},C^{'n}} \gamma_{n}$ exists in the configuration graph.
\end{proof}
\section{Appendix for Section \ref{sec:entailment}}\label{sec:append-ent}

We adapt the equivalence $\regeq_M$ of \cite{ouaknine2004language} to our
1-ATA setting. Fix a 1-ATA $\Aa$.

\begin{definition}[Region Equivalence]\label{def:regeq}
  Let $M \in \mathbb{N}$ be the largest constant appearing in
  $\Aa$. For two configurations $\gamma$ and $\gamma'$, we say that
  $\gamma$ is region equivalent to $\gamma'$, or that
  $\gamma \regeq_M \gamma'$, if we can define a bijection
  $h : \gamma \to \gamma'$ such that for every
  $(q,v),(q_{1},v_{1}),(q_{2},v_{2}) \in \gamma$:
  \begin{itemize}
  \item $\loc(h(q, v)) = q$,
  \item $\val(h(q, v)) = \bot$ iff $v = \bot$, \quad and \quad
    $0 \le \val(h(q, v)) \le M$ iff $0 \le v \le M$,
  \item if $0 \le v \le M$, then $\lfloor v \rfloor = \lfloor \val(h(q,v)) \rfloor$, and $\fract(v) = 0$ iff $\fract(\val(h(q,v))) = 0$,
  \item if $0 \le v_1, v_2 \le M$, then $\fract(v_1) \le \fract(v_2)$ iff
    $\fract(\val(h(q_1,v_1))) \le \fract(\val(h(q_2, v_2)))$.
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}
  For $\Aa_1$ in Fig. \ref{fig:ata}, we have $M = 1$. If 
  $\gamma_{1} = \{(q_{0},1.2),(q_{1},0),(q_2,1.2)\}$ and
  $\gamma_{2} = \{(q_0,1.5),(q_1,0),(q_0,0.3),(q_2,1.5)\}$,
  then $\gamma_{1} \entails_1 \gamma_{2}$ because taking subset
  $\gamma'_{2} \subseteq \gamma_{2}$ where
  $\gamma'_{2} = \{(q_0,1.5),(q_1,0),(q_2,1.5)\}$,
  $\gamma'_{2} \regeq_1 \gamma_{1}$ by the mapping $h$ with
  $h((q_{0},1.2)) = (q_0,1.5)$, $h((q_{1},0)) = (q_1,0)$ and
  $h((q_2,1.2)) = (q_2,1.5)$.
\end{example}

If two configurations are region equivalent, then for every
configuration that is reachable from one, we can find a region
equivalent configuration that is reachable by the other. This is more
formally stated below.

\begin{lemma}
  \label{lem:configb}
For configurations $\gamma_{1}$, $\gamma_{2}$, $\gamma_{3}$ such
  that $\gamma_{1} \regeq_M \gamma_{3}$ and
  $\gamma_{1} \xra{t,a,C} \gamma_{2}$, there exists a $t' \ge 0$ and
  configuration $\gamma_{4}$ such that
  $\gamma_{3} \xra{t',a,C} \gamma_{4}$ and
  $\gamma_{2} \regeq_M \gamma_{4}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
  We need to find a $t'$ such that we have
  $\gamma_{3} \xra{t'} \gamma'_{3} \xra{a,C} \gamma_{4}$ and
  $\gamma_{2} \regeq_M \gamma_{4}$. We do that by observing that we need
  a $t'$ such that if $\gamma_{1} \xra{t} \gamma'_{1}$ and
  $\gamma_{3} \xra{t'} \gamma'_{3}$, then
  $\gamma'_{1} \regeq_M \gamma'_{3}$. This is because if so, the the
  same $(a,C)$ transition will be possible from both $\gamma'_{1}$ and
  $\gamma'_{3}$, and thus the resulting
  $\gamma_{2} \regeq_M \gamma_{4}$. So we look at the timed transition
  $\gamma_{1} \xra{t} \gamma'_{1}$. Let
  $\gamma_{1} = \{(q_{1},v_{1}),\dots,(q_{n},v_{n})\}$, where
  $\fract(v_{1}) \leq \fract(v_{2}) \leq \dots \leq \fract(v_{k})$ and
  $v_{l} > M$ or $v_{l} = \bot$ for $k < l \leq n$. Then, as
  $\gamma_{1} \regeq_M \gamma_{3}$, there is a bijection
  $h : \gamma_{1} \mapsto \gamma_{3}$ such that if
  $h((q_{j},v_{j})) = (q_{i_{j}},v_{i_{j}})$ for $1 \leq j \leq n$,
  then $\lfloor v_{j} \rfloor = \lfloor v_{i_{j}} \rfloor$ for
  $1 \leq j \leq k$,
  $\fract(v_{i_{1}}) \leq \fract(v_{i_{2}}) \leq \dots \leq
  \fract(v_{i_{k}})$ and $v_{i_{l}} > M$ or $v_{i_{j}} = \bot$ for
  $k < l \leq n$. Now, for
  $\gamma'_{1} = \{(\sigma_{1},v_{1}+t),\dots,(\sigma_{n},v_{n}+t)\}$,
  for $k < l \leq n$, $x_{l}+t$ will still be $> M$ or $\bot$, and for
  the rest, the integral part will just be the existing value
  $+\, \lfloor t \rfloor$. To get a region equivalent $\gamma'_{2}$,
  we first need $\lfloor t' \rfloor = \lfloor t \rfloor$, and so we
  have the integral part of $t'$. But we still need to define the
  fractional part of $t'$. To do so, we look at the order of
  fractional parts of $\gamma'_{1}$. If $\fract(t) = 0$, the order in
  $\gamma'_{1}$ will be the same as $\gamma_{1}$. In this case, we can
  set $t'$ as simply $t$, and we will get
  $\gamma'_{1} \regeq_M \gamma'_{2}$. Otherwise, when
  $\fract(t) \neq 0$, the order of fractional parts in $\gamma'_{1}$
  will change, and we will have some $m$ such that
  $\fract(x_{m}+ t) \leq \dots \leq \fract(x_{k} + t) \leq
  \fract(x_{1} + t) \leq \dots \leq \fract(x_{m-1} + t)$. To ensure
  that $\gamma'_{2}$ maintains this same order, we choose a $t'$ such
  that
  $1 - \fract(x_{i_{m}}) \leq \fract(t') < 1 -
  \fract(x_{i_{m-1}})$. Thus, we get a $t'$ as required, and get
  $\gamma_{4}$ as $\gamma_{2} \xra{t',a} \gamma_{4}$ such that
  $\gamma_{2} \regeq_M \gamma_{4}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
  \label{lem:coninvsim}
For configurations $\gamma_{1}$, $\gamma_{2}$, $\gamma_{3}$ such
  that $\gamma_{3} \entails_M \gamma_{1}$ and
  $\gamma_{1} \xra{t,a,C} \gamma_{2}$, there exists a $t' \ge 0$ and a
  configuration $\gamma_{4}$ such that
  $\gamma_{3} \xra{t',a,C} \gamma_{4}$ and
  $\gamma_{4} \entails_M \gamma_{2}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
  If $\gamma_{3} \entails_M \gamma_{1}$, it means there is some
  $\gamma'_{1} \subseteq \gamma_{1}$ such that
  $\gamma_{3} \regeq \gamma'_{1}$. Let
  $\gamma'_{1} \xra{t,a,C} \gamma'_{2}$. Now, from Lemma
  \ref{lem:configb}, we know that there is some $t'$ and $\gamma_{4}$
  such that $\gamma_{3} \xra{t',a,C} \gamma_{4}$ and
  $\gamma_{4} \regeq \gamma'_{2}$. But by definition,
  $\gamma'_{2} \subseteq \gamma_{2}$. This means
  $\gamma_{4} \entails_M \gamma_{2}$.
\end{proof}


\begin{lemma}
  \label{lem:zoneent}
For zones $(Z,\Inact)$ and $(Z',\Inact)$, if
  $(Z,\Inact) \entails_M (Z',\Inact)$ and $(Z',\Inact')$ has a path to
  an accepting zone in the zone graph, then so does $(Z,\Inact)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
  Let $(Z,\Inact) \entails_M (Z',\Inact')$ and let $(Z',\Inact')$ have a
  path to an accepting zone $(Z'_{a},\Inact_{a})$. This means that
  there is some $\gamma'_{a} \in (Z'_{a},\Inact'_{a})$ such that
  $\gamma'_{a}$ is accepting. By the successor computation on zones,
  this would mean that there is some $\gamma' \in (Z',\Inact')$ and a
  path
  $\gamma' \xra{t_{1},a_{1},,C^1} \gamma'_{1} \dots \gamma'_{n-1}
  \xra{t_{n},a_{n},C^n} \gamma'_{a}$. Now, as
  $(Z,\Inact) \entails_M (Z',\Inact')$, there is some
  $\gamma \in (Z,\Inact)$ such that $\gamma \entails_M \gamma'$. From
  Lemma \ref{lem:coninvsim}, we know that we can get
  $t'_{1},\dots t'_{k}$ and
  $\gamma_{1}, \dots ,\gamma_{k-1},\gamma_{a}$ such that
  $\gamma \xra{t'_{1},a_{1},C^1} \gamma_{1} \dots \gamma_{n-1}
  \xra{t'_{n},a_{n},C^n} \gamma_{a}$ and
  $\gamma_{i} \entails_M \gamma'_{i}$ for $1 \leq i \leq n-1$ and
  $\gamma_{a} \entails_M \gamma'_{a}$. This means there is some zone
  $(Z_{a},\Inact_{a})$ such that there is a path from $(Z,\Inact)$ to
  $(Z_{a},\Inact_{a})$ in the zone graph and
  $\gamma_{a} \in (Z_{a},\Inact_{a})$. Also, as
  $\gamma_{a} \entails_M \gamma'_{a}$ and $\gamma'_{a}$ is accepting, so
  is $\gamma_{a}$, and so $(Z_{a},\Inact_{a})$ is accepting. Thus
  there is a path to an accepting zone from $(Z,\Inact)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Algorithm for Entailment Check}

\paragraph*{Computing $N_{r}$}
We use the notion of Distance Graphs\cite{herbreteau2016better} for zones and regions in the computation of $N_{r}$ and the following result:

\begin{lemma}[\cite{herbreteau2016better}]\label{lem:nvecycle}
  A distance graph $G$ has some negative cycle iff $\llbracket G \rrbracket = \emptyset$.
\end{lemma}

Coming back to $N_{r}$ for a given $r: Var(Z) \mapsto Var(Z')$, we know that some $v' \in Z'_{r}$ is in $N_{r}$ when $v \not\regeq_M v'$ for every $v \in Z$. In other words, looking at the region of $v'$, $reg(v')$, then $reg(v') \not\in Reg(Z)$, where $Reg(Z)$ is the set of regions present in $Z$. Also, by this definition, if $v' \in N_{r}$ for some $v' \in Z'_{r}$, then for any $v'' \in Z'_{r}$ with $v'' \regeq_M v'$, even $v'' \in N_{r}$. So it is enough to check a representative valuation from each region in $Z'_{r}$.
As we have renamed the variables of $Z$ and $Z'_{r}$, we can directly talk about the distance graphs for $Z$ and regions of $Z'_{r}$. This means if $G_{Z}$ and $G_{reg(v')}$ are the distance graphs for $Z$ and the region of $v' \in Z_{r}$ respectively, then $v' \in N_{r}$ iff $G_{Z} \cap G_{reg(v')} = \emptyset$. From \ref{lem:nvecycle}, we know that it means $v' \in N_{r}$ iff $G_{Z} \cap \gamma_{reg(v')}$ has a negative cycle. Now, we use another result from \cite{herbreteau2016better}, modifying it from the context of LU-simulations to the maximum constant $M$ we use here.
\begin{lemma}[\cite{herbreteau2016better}]\label{lem:2cyc}
  $G_{Z} \cap G_{reg(v')}$ has a negative cycle iff there are two variables $y,y'$ such that:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item[-] $v'(y) \leq M$
    \item[-] If $v'(y') > M$, then $(<,-M) + (\leq, \lceil v'(y) \rceil) + (\lessdot_{yy'},c_{yy'}) < (\leq,0)$, and if $v'(y') \leq M$, then $(\leq,\lceil v'(y) \rceil - \lfloor v'(y') \rfloor) + (\lessdot_{yy'},c_{yy'}) < (\leq,0)$, where $y \xra{(\lessdot_{yy'},c_{yy'})} y'$ is the edge between $y,y'$ in $G_{Z}$.
  \end{itemize}
\end{lemma}

This means if there is a negative cycle in $G_{Z} \cap G_{reg(v')}$, there is a specific kind of negative cycle involving just 2 variables. This simplifies the way to compute $N_{r}$, as we can break it down into computing the valuations pairwise on all possible $y, y'$, then taking their union. If we define $N_{r,y,y'} = \{v' \in Z'_{r} \mid G_{Z} \cap G_{reg(v')} \text{ has a -ve cycle on }y,y'\}$, then we see that
\[N_{r} = \bigcup_{\forall y,y'} N_{r,y,y'} \text{ where }y,y' \in Var(Z)\cup \{0\} \]
Finally, we see that computing $N_{r,y,y'}$ is straightforward as we see from Lemma \ref{lem:2cyc} that $N_{r,y,y'}$ can be described as a zone. 
To compute this zone, we first build, for each $y,y'$, a distance graph $G_{y,y'}$ with vertices $Var(Z'_{r}) \cup \{0\}$ as follows (where $y \xra{(\lessdot_{yy'},c_{yy'})} y'$ is an edge in $G_{Z}$):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[-]We add edge $0 \xrightarrow{(\leq,\, M)} y$ to $G_{y,y'}$.
    \item[-]We add edge $y \xrightarrow{(<,\, c'_{yy'})} y'$ to $G_{y,y'}$.
    \item[-]If $(\leq,\, M) > (<, c'_{yy'} - M)$, we replace the previously added edge to $0 \xrightarrow{(<,\,c'_{yy'} - M)} y$ in $G_{y,y'}$.
    \item[-] We add edge $(<,\, \infty)$ between every other pair of variables in $G_{y,y'}$.
\end{enumerate}
The required $N_{r,y,y'} = G_{Z'_{r}} \cap G_{y,y'}$.


