\textit{Enhanced Ensemble Performance Analysis.} We evaluate ensembles of different sizes: 3-model ($\mathrm{C}_{24}^{3} = 2024$ combinations), 4-model ($\mathrm{C}_{24}^{4} = 10626$ combinations), and 5-model ($\mathrm{C}_{24}^{5} = 42504$ combinations). Table \ref{tab:aggregation_delta_stats} present the performance of CE-driven ensembles on OCRBench. Key findings reveal that as more models are integrated into the ensemble, the overall effectiveness improves significantly, evidenced by increasing $\Delta_{\max}$, $\Delta_{\min}$, and $\Delta_{\text{avg}}$ values with larger ensemble sizes. Specifically, CE consistently selects outputs that outperform the weakest and even the strongest models in the ensemble across all model combinations, as indicated by consistently positive $\Delta_{\min}$ values and almost non-existent negative $\Delta_{\max}$ occurrences. Notably, the $\Delta_{\min}^{-}$ Ratio and $\Delta_{\text{avg}}^{-}$ Ratio demonstrate that even in scenarios where the ensemble performance might not surpass the best individual model, the CE ensemble's outputs generally exceed the ensemble average and closely approach the performance of the best model. This trend is particularly pronounced in larger ensembles, where the $\Delta_{\text{avg}}^{-}$ Ratio approaches zero, highlighting CE's robustness and its ability to leverage collective model strengths effectively.

\textit{High-Performance \& Representative Ensemble Analysis.} Table~\ref{tab:model_combinations} presents the highest-scoring CE-driven ensembles, including both high-performing and notably interesting combinations. Several ensembles not only achieve scores beyond the current state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 926, but also demonstrate the potential of CE to enhance the capabilities of large models (over 70B parameters) and commercial closed-source models with the assistance of smaller models. \textbf{The best five-model ensemble reaches a score of 958}, while high-performing four- and three-model ensembles achieve scores of 955 and 941, respectively. Noteworthy is the performance of ensembles composed exclusively of lightweight open-source VLMs with fewer than 10 billion parameters. These ensembles can surpass the 926 benchmark, demonstrating CE's effectiveness in resource-constrained scenarios. For instance, \textbf{an ensemble of small open-source models (3B, 7B, 8B) surpasses the scores of commercial closed-source SOTA models like Step1o~\cite{Step-1V} and MUG-U-7B~\cite{MUG-U}}, as well as the open-source SOTA model Qwen2.5-VL-72B~\cite{Qwen2.5-VL}. This highlights the potential of CE to leverage smaller models to challenge and even outperform larger, more resource-intensive models.

