\section{Introduction}
The rapid development of large language models (LLMs) \citep{brown2020language, ouyang2022training, achiam2023gpt, touvron2023llama, touvron2023llama2, team2023gemini, reid2024gemini} is revolutionizing many fields, including software engineering \citep{ozkaya2023application, ebert2023generative}, robotics \citep{xie2023text2reward, ma2023eureka, chen2024rlingua}, and education \citep{jeon2023large, ansari2023mapping, kasneci2023chatgpt}. With substantial prior knowledge, LLMs can generate reasonable and reliable responses in various domains through appropriate prompt designs \citep{min2022rethinking, liu2023pre}. In the field of LLM-assisted education, existing works have demonstrated the usefulness of LLMs in programming education and coding understanding \citep{hellas2023exploring, nam2024using}, medical education \citep{safranek2023role}, mechanical engineering \citep{mustapha2024bard}, and chemical engineering education \citep{tsai2023exploring}, among others. It is important to note that the application of LLMs in educational fields requires careful evaluation of the models' capacities, as the inevitable hallucinations of LLMs \citep{huang2023survey, xu2024hallucination} may result in incorrect responses that mislead students. In this paper, we investigate how LLMs can be used in an undergraduate-level circuit analysis course, with the long-term aim of building a reliable and intelligent personalized virtual tutor for students majoring in electrical engineering \citep{rohde2024predictors}.

The learning materials for circuit analysis encompass various formats of information, including text, equations, and circuit diagrams \citep{svoboda2013introduction}. A primary challenge for leveraging LLMs in a circuit analysis course lies in their ability to reliably recognize and interpret this diverse information. Our experiment reveals that even state-of-the-art vision-language models (VLMs) and multimodal language models struggle to reliably interpret basic circuit diagrams, rendering them incapable of independently solving circuit analysis problems without external references. In this paper, we highlight one specific area where state-of-the-art LLMs can contribute to circuit analysis education: homework assessment. In this context, the reference solution typically contains sufficient information to address most problems. Therefore, we include the reference solution directly in the prompt, bypassing the need for LLMs to interpret circuit diagrams. To support this study, we developed a novel dataset comprising real student solutions in LaTeX format and used it to benchmark three LLMs, evaluating their capabilities in homework assessment for circuit analysis. \textit{To the best of our knowledge, this is the first exploration of how LLMs can assist in undergraduate-level circuit analysis education.}

The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows. 
\begin{itemize}
\item [i)] We construct a novel dataset that consists of 283 students' solutions from 119 problems on various topics in an undergraduate circuit analysis course. To facilitate the evaluation of current state-of-the-art LLMs, which are limited in their ability to recognize handwritten solutions, we handcrafted the dataset by converting both the official reference solutions and students' solutions to the LaTeX format using the Mathpix snipping tool. This dataset reflects the students' real homework completion behaviors in circuit analysis and serves as a reliable data source for evaluating the homework assessment capabilities of different LLMs.

\item [ii)] We test the homework assessment capabilities of three LLMs (GPT-3.5 Turbo, GPT-4o, and Llama 3 70B) using our dataset. In a designed prompt template, we ask the LLMs to assess student's homework solutions based on five different metrics: completeness, method, final answer, arithmetic error, and units. These metrics cover the main aspects in which students are prone to make mistakes in circuit analysis homework. We provide detailed results and systematic analyses of the performance of the considered LLMs in these metrics. We focus our analysis on the response characteristics of GPT-4o and Llama 3 70B, the better-performing options of the three models. This focus provides insights into the models' specific strengths and weaknesses in assessing homework for an undergraduate-level circuit analysis course. The evaluation framework proposed in this paper can also be generalized to a broader range of courses in engineering education.

\item [iii)] We discuss the limitations of current state-of-the-art LLMs in this context, including circuit diagram recognition, handwritten and typed solution recognition, and mathematical calculations. These identified limitations not only provide insights into strategies to navigate the limits of currently available LLMs for homework assessment but also suggest several possible improvement directions to build LLMs specific to the circuit analysis course, as well as a broader field of engineering education.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\label{S2}
This section provides the related work in the aspects of large language model evaluations, large language models for education, and automatic homework assessment. 

\textbf{Large Language Model Evaluations:} LLMs, as general-purpose language generation tools, have experienced rapid development in recent years, including advancements such as GPT-3 \citep{brown2020language}, Llama \citep{touvron2023llama}, Llama 2 \citep{touvron2023llama2}, and multimodal models like GPT-4 \citep{achiam2023gpt}, Gemini \citep{team2023gemini}, and Llama 3. In addition to technical reports released by model developers, researchers from various fields have evaluated the domain-specific performances of different LLMs. For instance, \cite{frieder2023mathematical} measured the mathematical capabilities of ChatGPT and GPT-4 using their collected datasets. Similarly, \cite{guo2023can} provided a comprehensive benchmark for five LLMs across eight chemistry tasks, and \cite{du2024mercury} assessed the computational efficiency of code LLMs. Other fields where LLMs have been evaluated include scientific literature analysis \citep{cai2024sciassess}, structured table data understanding \citep{sui2024table}, legal reasoning \citep{guha2024legalbench}, finance \citep{xie2024finben}, automated planning \citep{valmeekam2024planbench}, etc. With the long-term goal of creating a reliable personalized virtual tutor for students majoring in electrical engineering, we benchmark LLMs in this paper on homework assessment for an undergraduate-level circuit analysis course. 

\textbf{Large Language Models for Education:} 
Due to their vast internal knowledge, LLMs have the potential to provide personalized support for students in various domains \citep{kasneci2023chatgpt, jeon2023large}. Since the reliability of responses is essential in the field of education to avoid misleading students, many existing works have evaluated LLMs' capabilities across different disciplines, including mathematics \citep{frieder2023mathematical}, medicine \citep{thirunavukarasu2023large, liu2024benchmarking}, language learning \citep{baskara2023exploring}, business \citep{cribben2023benefits}, computational social science \citep{ziems2024can}, etc. Specifically, in the fields of engineering education \citep{qadir2023engineering}, LLM benchmarks have been established from different points of view in mechanical engineering \citep{tian2024assessing}, chemical engineering \citep{tsai2023exploring}, software engineering \citep{meissner2024evalquiz}, and control engineering \citep{kevian2024capabilities}. Notably, \citet{ngoc2023ai} evaluated the efficacy of ChatGPT in an integrated circuit systems course. The results indicated that ChatGPT can generate good responses for theoretical and fact-based questions but fails to produce reliable responses for questions whose answers include graphics. The dataset in \citep{ngoc2023ai} consists of only 24 questions, which might introduce biased results due to the small size. In our paper, we present a dataset consisting of 283 student's solutions from 119 problems and use it to evaluate the homework assessment capabilities of various LLMs, including the recently released GPT-4o and Llama 3 70B.

\textbf{Automatic Homework Assessment:} Prior to the advent of LLMs, automatic assessment systems were widely designed based on semantic comparisons between official solutions and students' solutions \citep{lan2015mathematical, liu2019automatic, aldriye2019automated}. The semantic comparisons in traditional automatic grading systems require discipline-specific feature extraction, limiting the system's performance and generalization due to the variety of students' solutions. Recent works have explored the potential of LLMs in enhancing automatic assessment systems with their versatile semantic analysis ability and massive internal knowledge \citep{fagbohun2024beyond, lee2024applying, xie2024grade}. 

Two popular subjects for LLM-assessment systems are essay writing and mathematical problem-solving \citep{yancey2023rating, botelho2023leveraging, xiao2024automation, li2024automated, yoo2024dress}. Essay writing is a purely natural language task, making it well suited for LLM assessment. Although mathematics relies on a broader skillset, LLMs such as GPT-4 have demonstrated the capability to handle mathematical tasks up to the undergraduate level \citep{frieder2023mathematical}. In this paper, we evaluate LLMs' capabilities in homework assessment for circuit analysis, a fundamental course for undergraduate students majoring in electrical engineering. Compared with the existing benchmarks in other disciplines, circuit analysis is challenging because it involves circuit diagrams, mathematics, and domain-specific circuit knowledge.

\section{Methods}
\label{S3}
In this section, we illustrate the methods used in this paper, including the collection of the dataset and the evaluation of the LLMs.

\subsection{Dataset Collections}
\label{S31}
We collected data from an undergraduate-level circuit analysis course in the School of Electrical and Computer Engineering at the Georgia Institute of Technology. Specifically, the raw data includes the assignment problems from the textbook \citep{svoboda2013introduction}, the official reference solutions, and the handwritten student solutions from 37 students. In detail, we have 283 student's solutions in total from 119 problems. We categorize the data into six topics, with the relationships between the topics and problem indices in \cite{svoboda2013introduction} shown in Table \ref{TabA1}, and descriptions of the topics and problems presented in Table \ref{TabA0}. These topics comprehensively cover the contents of an undergraduate-level circuit analysis course. 

