\section{LLM Evaluation Results}
\label{S4}
This section presents and analyzes the evaluation results of three LLMs on the dataset described in Section \ref{S31}. The tested LLMs include GPT-3.5 Turbo, GPT-4o, and Llama 3 70B\footnote{See the model descriptions at \url{https://platform.openai.com/docs/models/gpt-3-5-turbo} (GPT-3.5 Turbo), \url{https://platform.openai.com/docs/models/gpt-4o} (GPT-4o), and \url{https://github.com/meta-llama/llama3/blob/main/MODEL_CARD.md} (Llama 3 70B), respectively.}, which represent a basic baseline LLM, a state-of-the-art closed-source LLM, and a state-of-the-art open-source LLM, respectively. We leveraged the OpenAI API to get responses from GPT-3.5 Turbo and GPT-4o, and the Nvidia NIM API to obtain responses from Llama 3 70B\footnote{See \url{https://openai.com/api} (OpenAI API) and \url{https://build.nvidia.com/meta/llama3-70b} (Nvidia NIM API), respectively.}. To ensure the evaluation results are consistent and comparable, we used a common template for all three considered LLMs. Due to the free-form nature of the LLM responses, we \textit{manually} categorize the responses of LLMs into three classes: ``correct'', ``partially correct'', and ``incorrect'', based on the response's accuracy in assessing the student solution for each metric. The human rater evaluating the LLM responses is Liangliang Chen, the first author of this paper, who was also a teaching assistant for the circuit analysis course during the dataset collection period. In the assessment process, the rater carefully reviewed the LLM responses, categorizing them based on his expertise and the feedback he previously provided on students’ solutions in his role as a teaching assistant. Note that a response is considered partially correct if some sentences are accurate while others contain errors. Below, we present an example of a segment of a response classified as ``partially correct''. 

