\begin{itemize}
\item [i)] GPT-4o and Llama 3 70B significantly outperform GPT-3.5 Turbo across all metrics. Notably, GPT-4o achieves correct response rates exceeding 80\% on average across all metrics. This highlights the advancements in newly developed LLMs for homework assessment in the circuit analysis course. 

\item [ii)] GPT-4o demonstrates more consistent performance than Llama 3 70B, with a minimum correct response rate of 68.97\% across all topics and metrics. In contrast, Llama 3 70B falls below 60\% on the unit metric for three relatively advanced topics. 

\item [iii)] In the metrics of completeness and method, Llama 3 70B appears to outperform GPT-4o. Detailed analysis shows that Llama 3 70B tends to give positive evaluations of students' solutions, whereas GPT-4o is more rigorous. Consequently, GPT-4o has a higher false negative rate, while Llama 3 70B has a higher false positive rate. Since most students complete their solutions and use correct methods, GPT-4o is more likely to provide incorrect or partially incorrect responses for these two metrics. However, because students are more prone to omitting units, Llama 3 70B performs significantly worse than GPT-4o in the unit metric.

\item [iv)] The performance of LLMs varies across topics with different levels of difficulty. Sinusoidal steady-state analysis is the most challenging topic for both GPT-4o and Llama 3 70B, as indicated by the average percentage of correct responses. We speculate that this difficulty arises because steady-state analysis involves calculations and conversions of values across different forms, including complex numbers, phasors, and sinusoids. 
\end{itemize}

