\section{Limitations of LLMs in Circuit Analysis}
\label{S5}
Apart from the \textit{homework assessment} task discussed in Section \ref{S4}, this section discusses the limitations of current state-of-the-art LLMs/VLMs in \textit{solving circuit analysis problems}, using the results of GPT-4o as examples. On the one hand, the uncovered limitations can inform the future training of general LLMs or the fine-tuning of specific language models. On the other hand, they provide guidance on how to leverage the advantages and avoid the disadvantages of the currently available LLMs, which also shape the experiment design in this paper.


\subsection{Circuit Diagram Recognition and Understanding}
Circuit diagrams are key components in circuit analysis and the associated homework and solutions. Ideally, if a VLM can recognize the elements in a circuit diagram and understand their connections correctly, we can leverage this capability to extract the relevant prior knowledge from the VLM to provide useful instructions to students.

Unfortunately, GPT-4o, one of the state-of-the-art multimodal language models, cannot interpret a circuit diagram reliably. The following example shows that GPT-4o provides unreliable information when asked to list the elements in a circuit diagram and explain how these elements are connected. For the responses of GPT-4o in this section, we mark the incorrect parts with a \colorbox{red}{red} background and the dubious contents with a \colorbox{yellow}{yellow} background. We additionally mark with the \colorbox{green}{green} background the response segments that we would like to highlight as correct.


