In this example, GPT-4o is asked to identify the elements in an attached circuit diagram and explain how the elements are connected. In its response, GPT-4o mistakenly lists a non-existing resistor due to hallucinations. Regarding the element connections, GPT-4o's explanations are dubious and often incorrect. Since these diagrams contain most of the problem information\footnote{In this example, the text portion of the problem description is simply, ``Determine $i_{x}$ in the circuit below.''}, GPT-4o lacks the necessary context to evaluate a student's solution accurately if no additional information about the specific problem is provided. This is where the reference solutions, along with their final answers, play a critical role in the LLM prompt in Section \ref{S32}. They offer detailed information for specific problems and serve as reference benchmarks for homework assessment, enabling LLMs to understand what each problem requires without having to infer it. The failure of GPT-4o to recognize circuit diagrams also implies that we cannot use it to directly solve undergraduate circuit analysis problems, considering that most problem statements include one or more circuit diagrams. We further tested this aspect in the following example.

