			\begin{minipage}{0.6\textwidth}
				\[
				i_x = \frac{V_1 - 12.3}{10} = \frac{2.204 - 12.3}{10} \approx -1.01 \text{ A}
				\]
		\end{minipage}}
		~

		\colorbox{yellow}{Thus, \( i_x \) is approximately \(-1.01\) A.}
	\end{tcolorbox}
\end{center}

This example demonstrates that GPT-4o cannot solve a provided circuit analysis problem, which is expected since GPT-4o cannot even understand the associated circuit diagram. However, we note that GPT-4o does recognize that KCL should be applied to different nodes.

Due to the limitations of current LLMs/VLMs in circuit diagram recognition, we do not include any circuit diagrams in either the reference or student solutions when prompting an LLM to assess homework in this paper. The results in Section \ref{S4} demonstrate the relative effectiveness of our method without image inputs for both GPT-4o and Llama 3 70B.

\subsection{Handwritten and Typed Homework Solution Recognitions}
Other than circuit diagrams, it also appears to be a challenge for VLMs or multimodal language models like GPT-4o to reliably recognize handwritten and typed homework solutions. As shown in the examples in this section, there are errors when we simply prompt GPT-4o to extract the text in the attached image, either in handwritten or typed format, and print it literally in the responses.

The following example shows that the responses of GPT-4o include incorrect information when asked to extract the information from a \textit{handwritten} homework solution\footnote{This homework solution in this example was handwritten by Liangliang Chen, the first author of this paper.} and print it literally. This implies that we cannot use images of student handwritten solutions as direct inputs to GPT-4o for homework assessment. We also demonstrate the results recognized by the Mathpix snipping tool, which is better than that generated by GPT-4o.

