\section{Discussions and Future Directions}
This section presents the discussions of this paper and some future directions. 

\subsection{Reliability Improvement by Prompt Engineering and LLM Fine-tuning}
According to the results in Table \ref{Tab0} of Section \ref{S4}, LLMs, particularly GPT-4o, exhibit promising capabilities in homework assessment for the circuit analysis course. However, due to the critical need for reliability when providing assessment results to students, it is essential to explore strategies to enhance the reliability of LLMs through prompt engineering and model fine-tuning. In this paper, a common prompt template was used for all three evaluated LLMs to ensure fair comparisons of their responses. In future research, we plan to develop customized prompts tailored to each LLM to further optimize their performance in assessing homework. The limitations identified in this study provide valuable insights for refining prompt designs or fine-tuning LLMs with appropriate data. We are actively investigating approaches to improve off-the-shelf models for homework assessment in circuit analysis courses.

\subsection{Information Extraction from Circuit Diagrams}
In this paper, the official reference solution serves as a benchmark for student homework assessment. It also provides essential information for LLMs in conducting this assessment process. Circuit diagrams, however, are crucial elements in circuit analysis courses. Although the state-of-the-art model, GPT-4o, cannot yet reliably recognize elements in a basic circuit diagram, we can design and employ external tools to extract information from these diagrams and present it in a way that LLMs can interpret. This approach will enable LLMs to conduct assessment even when no reference solution is available. Future research can further explore the use of LLMs to independently solve circuit analysis problems. Additionally, it would be also worthwhile to investigate how to appropriately represent and process extracted information from circuit diagrams to enhance LLMs' comprehension \citep{hakhamaneshi2022pretraining, yamakaji2024circuit2graph, jin2024large}.

\subsection{Generalizability of the Evaluation Framework}
The dataset used in this paper is drawn solely from the circuit analysis course, and accordingly, the LLMs are evaluated only on homework assessment for this subject. In future work, it would be valuable to expand the dataset to include additional courses in electrical and computer engineering, as well as other areas within the broader engineering field. To generalize the evaluation framework to other domains, we may consider the intrinsic capabilities of LLMs, the construction of the dataset, and the selection of appropriate metrics. 

\begin{itemize}
\item If the LLMs to be evaluated are sufficiently powerful, they should ideally be able to generate reasonable homework assessments based solely on their intrinsic capabilities. Thus, when adapting the evaluation framework in this paper to other domains, it is advisable to first test a few examples to determine whether the evaluated LLMs possess adequate intrinsic capabilities for homework assessment without the support of additional datasets. If not, it would be necessary to evaluate the LLMs' performance by augmenting the prompts with appropriate external data, such as reference solutions.

\item To prepare evaluation data, it is important to consider whether diagrams are essential components of the problem descriptions and solutions. If they are, it is necessary to first assess whether LLMs can interpret these diagrams or whether alternative sources of information can be used to prompt the LLMs. In other words, datasets containing the necessary information for homework evaluation should be constructed to effectively leverage the evaluation framework. To further ensure \textit{reliable} homework assessment, it is crucial that the dataset provides sufficient information that can be readily understood by LLMs, either directly or through appropriate adaptation.

\item From a metric perspective, the evaluation criteria should be adapted to different domains when applying the proposed framework. In Section \ref{S32} of this paper, we selected five metrics---completeness, method, final answer, arithmetic, and unit---to evaluate LLM performance within the domain of undergraduate-level circuit analysis. However, these metrics should be modified to suit the specific contexts of other domains. Several existing studies have explored LLMs' assessment capabilities across various fields, which may serve as useful references for researchers and practitioners in other areas.

For instance, \cite{zhu2024impact} assessed the assignment completion ability of LLMs in the context of middle school students’ learning, using a scoring rubric that included criteria such as completion, creativity, accuracy, material refinement, depth, logic, and page aesthetics. Similarly, \cite{chiang2023can} evaluated LLM-generated open-ended stories based on grammaticality, cohesiveness, likability, and relevance. In another example, \cite{wang2024enhancing} examined LLM performance in Python code generation using various prompt-engineering strategies, with evaluations focusing on metrics such as pass rate, time spent, and Pylint score. In summary, the choice of evaluation metrics and associated prompt designs is task-specific and remains flexible in the evolving landscape of LLM development.
\end{itemize} 

\section{Conclusion}
\label{S6}
In this paper, we benchmark three LLMs, specifically GPT-3.5 Turbo, GPT-4o, and Llama 3 70B, on homework assessment for an undergraduate-level circuit analysis course. The LLMs are evaluated using five different metrics on our introduced dataset composed of real student solutions and official solutions. The results demonstrate that GPT-4o and Llama 3 70B can generate relatively reasonable homework assessments, with their reliability varying according to the difficulty of different topics. We summarize the strengths and weaknesses of GPT-4o and Llama 3 70B. Our experiments also reveal GPT-4o's limitations in understanding circuit diagrams, recognizing handwritten and typed homework solutions, and performing independent mathematical calculations. Our future work will investigate how to build a personalized tutor in circuit analysis by leveraging the advantages of LLMs and circumventing their weaknesses identified in this paper.

