Note that the dataset used in this paper will not be shared with the community, as the students enrolled in the Fall 2021 circuit analysis course did not sign a consent form, and their solutions are integral to this dataset. However, we are actively collecting additional student homework data from various courses, including circuit analysis. In future publications, we plan to release datasets collected from students who have provided consent.

The basic elements in the dataset are structured based on different students' solutions to the associated problems. The problems in our dataset are all from the textbook \citep{svoboda2013introduction}, and the students' solutions are collected from a circuit analysis course for undergraduates involving 37 students in the School of Electrical and Computer Engineering at the Georgia Institute of Technology. The folders of these basic elements are named with the following method, in which we take \texttt{q\_010502\_cs\_5} as an example to illustrate.  

\begin{itemize}
	\item [i)] In this example, ``\texttt{q\_010502}'' means that the corresponding problem is Problem 1.5-2 in \citep{svoboda2013introduction}. Specifically, the codes ``\texttt{01}'' and ``\texttt{05}'' indicate the chapter and section indexes, respectively, while ``\texttt{02}'' is the problem index in the section.
	\item [ii)] The code ``\texttt{cs\_5}'' indicates that the included student's solution in the folder \texttt{q\_010502\_cs\_5} is from the student with index \texttt{5}. The student indices are mainly for our internal use to track the original documents of students' solutions. They are de-identified and consistently used across different problems. Additionally, ``\texttt{cs}'' implies that student \texttt{5}'s solution is considered correct in all metrics. We use the code ``\texttt{ws}'' to represent the students' solutions that have errors in at least one metric out of the five considered. For example, ``\texttt{q\_010502\_ws\_31}'' indicates that the solution from student \texttt{31} solves Problem 1.5-2 in \citep{svoboda2013introduction} with at least one error in one aspect.
\end{itemize}


The following data are included in the basic data folder like \texttt{q\_010502\_cs\_5}.
\begin{itemize}
	\item \texttt{raw\_official\_solution.txt}: This file contains the official solution to the corresponding problem in LaTeX format. The contents of this file are inserted in the LLM prompt when we leverage LLMs to assess students' homework. 

	\item \texttt{official\_solution.pdf}: For a better reading experience, we generated a PDF file corresponding to the official solution \texttt{raw\_official\_solution.txt}.

	\item \texttt{handwritten\_student\_solution.png}: This is the original source file of the student's solution. To test the capabilities of LLMs in homework assessment, we converted this image to a LaTeX document using the Mathpix snipping tool. The converted results are saved in the file \texttt{raw\_student\_solution.txt}. 

	\item \texttt{raw\_student\_solution.txt}: This file contains the student's solution in LaTeX format. The contents of this file are included in the prompt and evaluated by the tested LLMs.

	\item \texttt{student\_solution.pdf}: For a better reading experience, we generated a PDF file corresponding to the student's solution \texttt{raw\_student\_solution.txt}. 

	\item \texttt{final\_answer.txt}: This file contains a short summary of the final answer to the corresponding problem. Comparing final answers is an easy task for the LLM, and a student's incorrect final answer may motivate the LLM to identify where the student made the error. The contents of this file are also included as part of the LLM prompt. 

	The final answer provides a concise summary of the official solution, omitting the problem-solving process. For example, a circuit problem may ask students to determine (a) the current $i_{R}$ through a resistor $R$, (b) the voltage $v_{R}$ across the resistor, and (c) the power $p_{R}$ absorbed by this resistor. The final answer might be presented as: ``(a) $i_{R} = 1~\mathrm{A}$; (b) $v_{R} = 1~\mathrm{V}$; (c) $p_{R} = 1~\mathrm{W}$.'' In contrast, the official solution includes a detailed derivation of these values, showing how relevant circuit laws and analysis methods are applied.

	Including the final answer in the prompt helps LLMs avoid overlooking important information when assessing student solutions. This is particularly useful when evaluating metrics such as completeness and accuracy of final answers, especially in problems with multiple sub-parts, like the example in the previous paragraph.

	\item \texttt{responses}: This file consists of the responses from the three LLMs evaluated in this paper. The evaluation results are structured into three subfolders: \texttt{gpt-3.5-turbo-0125}, \texttt{gpt-4o-2024-05-13}, and \texttt{llama3-70b-instruct}, according to the models. Each subfolder contains two files: \texttt{response.txt}, which saves the direct LLM output, and \texttt{response.pdf}, which is the corresponding PDF file of \texttt{response.txt} for a better reading experience. The evaluation classification results are obtained by human inspection based on the file \texttt{response.pdf}. 
\end{itemize}


