Recently, large language models (LLMs) demonstrate exceptional capabilities in generating human-like text, leveraging pretraining on extensive datasets \cite{radford2018improving}. These capabilities enable LLMs to potentially address the shortcomings of earlier approaches by producing nuanced, contextually relevant, and adaptable content, making them particularly well-suited for educational purposes. %For generating more topic-specific content, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a powerful technique \cite{lewis2020retrieval, gao2023retrieval}. RAG-based LLM content generation combines document retrieval with text generation, ensuring that generated content aligns with specific knowledge sources \cite{lewis2020retrieval, gao2023retrieval}. 
However, generating comprehensive lessons for tutor training remains a complex task. A lesson typically consists of multiple interconnected segments, including well-defined learning objectives and quiz. Each segment serves a specific instructional purpose and must align with the overall pedagogical goals of the lesson. %Furthermore, the lesson generation process should follow a logical sequence\textemdash for example, introducing the learning objectives at the outset, providing instructional content as the core of the lesson, and then incorporating interactive questions and feedback to reinforce understanding and assess comprehension. This structured progression is critical to ensure that the lesson content aligns with the intended learning objectives and maintains coherence across sections. 
Given the complexity of lesson design, generating all components in a single step risks overwhelming the model, resulting in shallow or disjointed outputs \cite{khot2022decomposed}. %To address this, a decomposed prompting strategy can be employed to break the task of lesson generation into smaller more manageable sub-tasks. 



To address this, we adopt a prompting strategy similar to \cite{wu2022ai}, chaining multiple LLM steps together\textemdash where the output of one step serves as the input for the next\textemdash and has been shown to enhance the quality of task outcomes. Research highlights the benefits of such decomposition strategies for tasks requiring multi-step reasoning, as they help improve the quality of generated content \cite{wu2022ai, khot2022decomposed}. However, it remains unknown how extensively a task should be decomposed to ensure optimal performance, since overly complex sub-goals might still overwhelm the LLM \cite{li2024towards}. In the context of lesson generation, this challenge becomes particularly relevant, as lessons consist of multiple interdependent components that must align both structurally and pedagogically. It remains unclear how the number of segments or the level of decomposition impacts the quality, coherence, and pedagogical rigor of the generated lessons. 


