\section{Methods}
\vspace{-2mm}

\subsection{Lesson Structure from a Tutor Training Platform}
\label{lesson_structure}
\vspace{-1mm}
To develop training lessons for novice human tutors teaching middle school mathematics online, our study builds upon an existing tutor training program structured around the SMART framework demonstrated in \cite{chhabra2022evaluation, thomas2023tutor}, which encompasses five key competencies critical for successful tutoring: \textbf{S}ocial-emotional Learning, \textbf{M}astery of Content, \textbf{A}dvocacy, \textbf{R}elationship-building, and \textbf{T}echnology. All lessons in our study were generated in English.
%Currently, the platform offers over 20 tutor training lessons, with each lesson designed to target one or more of these competencies. The lessons engage tutors through scenario-based questions that require them to answer predicted and explained questions related to real-world tutoring. Tutors participate in pre- and post-tests that feature similar scenarios to help assess their learning progress \cite{chhabra2022evaluation, thomas2023tutor}. The effectiveness of these scenario-based lessons has been well-documented, showing significant gains in tutor performance from pretests to posttests, demonstrating the efficacy of the instructional design \cite{thomas2023tutor}. This approach relies on the learn-by-doing pedagogy, which is well known to promote skill acquisition through active participation \cite{srinivasa2022learning}. As such, human tutors on our platform benefit from structured, interactive app-based lessons, which play an essential role in both their onboarding and ongoing professional development. 
The lessons are divided into five main sections, each designed to support specific aspects of tutor training:
    \vspace{-2mm}



\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Title Page:} This section provides a description of the lesson topic, including the key focus areas and the learning objectives the lesson aims to achieve. %The learning objectives are clearly detailed so that tutors understand the skills they are expected to develop by the end of the lesson. For instance, a lesson may focus on understanding how to give effective feedback in a tutoring session, with specific objectives such as being able to deliver timely and meaningful praise.
    \item \textbf{Scenario I Section:} In this section, tutors are presented with the first tutoring scenario and are required to answer open-ended questions and multiple-choice questions (MCQ). These questions are designed to replicate real-world tutoring situations, asking tutors to respond as they would during an actual session and to justify their responses. %Scenario-based questions often require tutors to address a specific student issue, such as perseverance during a difficult assignment. For example, tutors might be presented with a scenario in which a student is struggling to complete a math assignment, and they must provide responses that reflect effective praise strategies, followed by an explanation for why their approach would be beneficial. 
    \item \textbf{Instruction:} This section offers research-backed instruction for effective tutoring practices, helping tutors understand both desired and undesired tutoring practice. Each instruction section includes specific examples that help clarify best practices. 
    \item \textbf{Scenario II Section:} This section follows a structure similar to the \textbf{Scenario I} section but presents a different scenario that requires tutors to apply their learned skills in a slightly altered context. This variation ensures that tutors understand the applicability of concepts beyond a single situation. 
    \item \textbf{Conclusion:} The lesson concludes with a summary that reviews the key concepts covered throughout the lesson, reinforcing the understanding of tutoring strategies discussed. The conclusion section also includes a list of references, providing tutors with additional resources for deeper exploration% of the topics. 
    \vspace{-3mm}
\end{itemize}


\subsection{Human-Crafted Lesson}
\vspace{-1mm}
We invited two lesson designers to craft the lessons for tutor training and both lesson designers followed the structured lesson format outlined in Section \ref{lesson_structure}. Each designer was tasked with creating lessons aligned with specific tutoring scenarios. The first lesson designer developed two lessons. The first lesson is \textit{``Encouraging Students' Independence''} which focuses on fostering student autonomy by training tutors to use polite, constructive language to guide learners without undermining their independence and the second lesson is \textit{``Turning on Cameras''}, which aims to equip novice tutors with strategies to encourage camera use in online tutoring sessions, aiming to improve student engagement and active participation. The second lesson designer developed one lesson, i.e., \textit{``Encouraging Help-Seeking Behavior''}, which aims to train tutors in identifying and addressing barriers that prevent students from seeking help, fostering a more supportive learning environment. All three lessons underwent rigorous review and multiple iterations, ensuring they were polished and ready for publication on the tutor training platform. The topics of these three human-crafted lessons were subsequently used to prompt the LLMs, following the prompt engineering approaches detailed in Section \ref{prompt_engineering}. The human-crafted lessons are publicly accessible through the  \href{https://osf.io/wafnm/files/osfstorage?view_only=e6dfcb2c31854245b4882621f7469be5}{OSF repository}
\vspace{-3mm}

\subsection{Large Language Model-Generated Lessons}
\label{prompt_engineering}
% \vspace{-1mm}
We aim to generate comprehensive tutor training lessons based on research articles related to effective tutoring practices. For generating more topic-specific content, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a powerful technique \cite{lewis2020retrieval, gao2023retrieval}. RAG-based LLM content generation combines both retrieval and generation capabilities, allowing the model to first retrieve relevant documents and then utilize the relevant information to generate content. The strength of RAG lies in its ability to produce contextually accurate content by incorporating the generated output into factual data, which is particularly advantageous when generating educational content that requires specific knowledge from reliable sources \cite{rao2024ramo}. As shown in Figure \ref{lesson_generation}, we propose a two-step approach for lesson generation. Firstly, a lesson designer retrieves articles related to specific tutoring practices, providing the foundational knowledge on effective instructional strategies. Secondly, these retrieved articles are used to prompt the GPT-4o model (\texttt{gpt-4o-2024-05-13}). The GPT-4o model has been recognized for its balance of cost efficiency and strong performance in content generation tasks, making it a suitable choice for producing pedagogically sound lesson content.





An important aspect of generating tutor training lessons is determining the optimal prompting strategy for content generation. As discussed in Section \ref{lesson_structure}, each lesson consists of five main sections. %: a title page, scenario I section, instruction, scenario II section, and a conclusion. 
  It is important to explore whether it is more effective to generate an entire lesson in one step or generate multiple segments of lesson section (e.g., generate each section independently). %This exploration leads us to our \textbf{RQ1}. \textit{How does the level of task decomposition influence the quality of tutor training lessons generated by LLMs?} To address this research question, 
  %We used the RAG approach to investigate which segment configurations perform best in terms of lesson quality. 
  As shown in Figure \ref{lesson_generation}, the lesson structure includes five sections, and each section can be generated using different prompting strategies. Additional details about our prompting strategies can be accessed through the  \href{https://osf.io/wafnm/files/osfstorage?view_only=e6dfcb2c31854245b4882621f7469be5}{OSF repository}

% [Add]
% We first give the prompt to the retriever, in the prompt, we mention the grade level of our target student -- grade 6-8 students in US learning math. %Besides, we also mention Flesch–Kincaid readability tests are 9 for enhancing the readibility of the course. 

% As for the prompts, we also emphasize the  bloom's taxonomy :understand and create in our prompt design.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{One Segment:} The entire lesson (all five sections) is generated at once. %This comprehensive approach might ensure consistency throughout the lesson. However, this approach often sacrifices detail and specificity in individual sections. The complex prompting statement placed on the model when generating all content simultaneously can lead to generic or shallow outputs for the whole lesson, as the model struggles to allocate sufficient attention to each section.
    \item \textbf{Two Segments:} The lesson is split into two main segments for generation. %: Segment 1 (S1): Title page, Scenario I, and instruction and Segment 2 (S2): Scenario II and conclusion. 
    The content from S1, including the title page and the description of Scenario I, is incorporated as part of the prompt for generating S2. %By splitting the lesson into two segments, this approach allows the model to generate more detailed and focused content compared to the one-segment approach. However, while this method improves depth in each section, it might introduce minor inconsistencies or logical gaps between the two segments, particularly in the transition from Scenario I to Scenario II.
    \item \textbf{Three Segments:} The lesson was divided into three segments. %: (S1) the title page and scenario I, (S2) the instruction, and (S3) scenario II and the conclusion. 
    The content from S1 is used to prompt the generation of the instructional content (S2), while both S1 and S2 are incorporated as inputs to generate Scenario II and the conclusion (S3). %This approach strikes a balance between coherence and detail. By isolating the instruction section, the model can focus on generating pedagogically rich and contextually grounded content without compromising the logical flow of the lesson. Compared to the two-segment approach, the three-segmeent approach could potentially enhance clarity and depth in the instruction section while maintaining overall cohesion. However, the sequential nature of generation for Scenario II and the conclusion might result in slight overlaps or redundancies.
    \item \textbf{Four Segments:} The lesson was split into four segments. %: (1) title page, (2) Scenario I, (3) instruction, and (4) Scenario II and conclusion. %This approach builds on the strengths of the three-segment strategy, particularly isolating the instruction section to ensure detailed, pedagogically grounded content. 
    Since the instruction section includes descriptions of research-recommended tutoring practices, generating it independently enhances clarity and depth. Scenario II and the Conclusion are generated together to streamline transitions. 
    \item \textbf{Five Segments:} The most granular approach involves generating each of the five segments. %: (S1) title page, (S2) Scenario I, (S3) Scenario II, (S4) instruction, and (S5) conclusion. 
    This approach provided the highest level of detail and customization for each section, allowing the model to focus exclusively on the objectives of a single segment. %However, this approach often comes at the expense of coherence. The frequent need to reestablish context for each new segment increases the risk of redundancies and inconsistencies across sections. 
\end{itemize}



