\emph{The use of precise, repeatable experiments to validate any claim is  the hallmark of a mature scientific or engineering discipline.}-- Lewis, Henry et al 1991\footnote{http://eprints.cs.vt.edu/archive/00000253/01/TR-91-06.pdf}

Theoretical Computer Science is vitally important and beautiful, but in the large Computer Science is an experimental discipline.  The most important theoretical results obtain their impact from experiment.  For example, the question P=NP? derives its extreme interest from the fact that NP-complete problems are of major practical importance, and that the distinction between polynomial and non-polynomial runtime turns out to be  vital in practice.   P=NP would be of much less interest if it turned out, empirically, that most P problems we came across had runtimes like $n^{1000}$.


If there is one thing computers are good at, it is doing things over and over again.    It's time that the discipline of Computer Science did things over and over again.  Much of our scientific knowledge about computers comes from experimentation.  Replication of experiments is fundamental to making scientific knowledge secure.  The nature of computers means that experiments are inherently easy to replicate.   It is extraordinary, then, that replication of experiments in Computer Science is almost entirely absent.   
This must change.

