\subsection{Deterministic predictions} Motivated by the observation of \citet{charton2024learning} that models predict the same incorrect number deterministically, we compare predictions and targets for various model checkpoints. The model trained on reciprocal operands, checkpointed upon reaching validation accuracy 30\%, reliably predicts the number 19 instead of the correct 91. For all 10012 generated samples with target 91, the model predicted 19. These samples contain duplicates --- the 10012 samples are made up of 512 distinct ones.
Upon isolating this effect by rejection sampling $a,b,c$ with fixed $d=91$ and duplicate elimination, and scaling up the overall sample count, prediction 1 becomes the most common for target 91. The 19 is still present for common inputs, while the 1 is used for infrequent $a,b,c$. We hypothesize that the 1 might serve as a fall-back mechanism for our model, especially relevant for unseen and rare data.


\subsection{Learning Dynamics Analysis}
During training, we observe a significant surge in performance between epochs 1725 and 1750, as seen in \autoref{fig:validcomparison}. When decomposed number-wise (by modulus), we observe individual increases from 20\% to 100\% accuracy for the moduli 92, 69, 46, 23 --- the multiples of 23 in the range of the parameter $c \in [1, 100]$. The overall increase in accuracy can therefore be explained by the simultaneous stepwise increases in accuracy for certain moduli. A similar effect is observed with the multiples of 31, 39, and 47. Some further visualizations of this phenomenon can be found in appendix \ref{append:moduli}. 
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{validcomparison.png}
    \caption{Sudden increase in performance for all four multiples of 23 in the allowed range of the modulus. The increase starts at epoch 1725 and stops once the moduli have reached 100\% accuracy at epoch 1750.}
    \label{fig:validcomparison}
\end{figure}
A few small moduli, namely 1, 2, 4, 10, 12, 14, 15, and 18, are learned within the first 100 epochs to an accuracy of 100\%. The rest exhibits a period of low accuracy, before increasing rapidly for a few epochs and then leveling off near perfect accuracy. This leads to an overall stepwise increase in accuracy, whenever a new modulus is solved. This is similar to the learning dynamics described by \citet{charton2024learning} on the GCD task.
The surges are not observed for the other operands $a,b$ or the outcome $d$. This numberwise grokking, especially for multiples at the same time, suggests that the model learns a mathematically meaningful function. Since multiples increase at the same time, we hypothesize that the numerical representations must capture this effect. Therefore, we explore whether the underlying representations encapsulate relations between integers.

