\paragraph{Numeric Tokens}
We analyzed embeddings initially by numeric value. Prior to grokking, the model forms 6 spatially distinct clusters with no clear linear mapping of token value. After grokking, embeddings become more centered and compressed, especially for lower numbers.

\paragraph{Lowest Prime Factor}
We applied PCA on numeric token embeddings colored by their lowest prime factor, drawing inspiration from the analysis by \citet{charton2024learning}. Before grokking, tokens with small prime factors show mild localized clustering. After grokking, the tokens seem to cluster around the center.

\paragraph{Parity}
Visualizing numeric token embeddings by parity is insightful because even numbers inherently share the lowest prime factor of 2, creating a natural partition in modular arithmetic. Before grokking, our PCA analysis shows little clear separation between even and odd numbers, with post-grokking tokens appearing more centralized.

\paragraph{Prime}
We visualized the PCA embeddings by prime numbers versus composites and did not observe any clearly distinct clusters before grokking and one larger cluster after grokking. 

\paragraph{Divisor Count}
We colored each numeric token by its total number of divisors to capture a more fine-grained view of compositeness. Before grokking, tokens with low divisor counts are weakly distinguishable, but after grokking they seem to compress towards a central region. 

\paragraph{Multiplicative Order}
We visualized multiplicative order a modulo k (the smallest positive integer k such that $a^{k} \equiv 1 \mod k$, assuming that a and k are coprime). We computed this for tokens that are coprime to 7 and colored them by their order. The embedding space does not seem to sharply cluster by multiplicative order before grokking, and again appears more centralized after grokking. 

\paragraph{Euler's Totient/Theorem}
We used Euler's totient function $\phi(n)$, which counts the number of integers less than $n$ that are coprime to $n$. In number theory and cryptography, $\phi(n)$ is fundamental to results such as Euler's theorem and RSA encryption, where $a^{\phi(n)} \equiv 1 \mod{n}$ for coprime $a$. We computed $\phi(n)$ for each numeric token and visualized it as a color gradient over PCA embeddings. In our plots, $\phi(n)$ values form slight gradients in some clusters before grokking and after-grokking seem to cluster towards the center. 

\paragraph{Primitive Root}
In modular exponentiation, a primitive root modulo a prime p is a number whose successive powers generate all non-zero residues mod p. We label tokens by whether they are primitive roots (1) or not (0) for a fixed prime (11) and apply PCA to see if those “generators” form distinct embedding patterns. Before grokking, embeddings appear loosely scattered, while after grokking they seem to converge inwards.

\paragraph{Residue}
Finally, we color tokens by their residue class modulo a small integer (i.e., 5) to check if the embedding space captures simple mod-N structure. While residue appears somewhat mixed across pre-grokking clusters, residues appear more centralized post grokking. 

\paragraph{Multiples}
To investigate whether the model captures multiplicative structure, we color multiples of 23, 31, and 39. Although some same-multiple tokens loosely co-locate before grokking, no clear structure emerges post-grokking. 


\subsection{Activation Patching}
Activation patching is a mechanistic interpretability method that overwrites activation values in some component of the model \citep{heimersheim2024useinterpretactivationpatching}. This is done to isolate which parts of the model have a causal effect when removed. We conduct two patching experiments to identify minimal circuits within our trained model, replacing activations of a given input with the activations from a counterfactual prompt. We follow the methodology of \citet{conmy2023towards} to select our circuit. For each decoder layer, we run the counterfactual prompt through the network and capture the self-attention context (the input to the output projection) using forward hooks. For each attention head, we run the full input through the network, while replacing the head’s activations with the corresponding counterfactual context. We then compute the KL divergence between the logits of the patched and unpatched network outputs. Heads for which the KL divergence exceeds a fixed threshold ($\tau$) are considered critical and are included in the final circuit. When evaluating the circuit's effect, we simultaneously patch all the selected heads. 

\paragraph{Regular Exponentiation}
 The most natural capability that one would expect from a model trained on modular exponentiation is the ability to perform regular exponentiation. In our experiments, when the model is prompted with inputs for which $c > a^b$ he target output is simply $a^b$ (i.e. no modulo reduction occurs). We guarantee nontrivial modular exponentiation by selecting $c$ smaller than $a^b$. We generate 100 such prompt-counterfactual pairs.

 \begin{figure}
     \centering
     \includegraphics[width=0.45\linewidth]{kl_divergence_heatmap.png}
     \label{fig:enter-label}
    \includegraphics[width=0.45\linewidth]{accuracy_comparison.png}
    \caption{KL divergence heatmap and of attention heads across generated samples (left) and comparison of accuracy between full and patched model (right).}
 \end{figure}

After circuit search, we find a circuit composed of all heads in the latest layer with the same accuracy as the full model. We vary $\tau \in (0.01, 0.05, 0.1, 0.5, 1, 2)$, finding that many early layers' heads are not selected --- suggesting that their activations have little causal impact on the final computation.

The final circuit is able to achieve high accuracy on the task, suggesting that the model meaningfully learns an internal representation of a task that is not modular exponentiation. In addition, the circuit is a substantially smaller fraction of the original model, which confirms our hypothesis that exponentiation mainly relies on the latest decoder layers. These findings align with the intuition that higher-layer heads encode abstract, task-specific transformations necessary for exponentiation, whereas lower-layer heads capture more generic or redundant information.

