Since we also have edge $(y,w)$, therefore $\mathbf{y}\in T(w)$.    So we have shown that
$\beta(\mathbf{y})\supseteq \{y,x,w\}$, and equality holds since $G$ contains no 4-clique.

Vertex $z$ is adjacent to $x$, but not in $\beta(\mathbf{x})$, 
so $T(x) \overshadows T(z)$.   Therefore $\mathbf{z}\in T(x)$ is a strict descendant of $\mathbf{x}$,
hence also of $\mathbf{r}$.
Since we also have edge $(z,r)$, therefore $\mathbf{z}\in T(r)$.    So we have shown that
$\beta(\mathbf{z})\supseteq \{z,x,r\}$, and equality holds since $G$ contains no 4-clique.

Recall that $T(w)\overshadows T(x)$. Its cutoff value must be at least $d(\mathbf{y})$
(since $\mathbf{y}\in T(x)\cap T(w)$) but strictly smaller than $d(\mathbf{z})$ (since $\mathbf{z}\in T(x)\setminus T(w)$).
In particular therefore $d(\mathbf{z})> d(\mathbf{y})$.   We also know that $\mathbf{z}$ cannot be a 
descendant of $\mathbf{y}$, since otherwise $\mathbf{y}$ would lie on the path from $\mathbf{z}$
to $\mathbf{x}$ and $\beta(\mathbf{y})$ would have to contain $r$.  So along
the path from $\mathbf{x}$ to $\mathbf{z}$, we somewhere must have the same depth as $\mathbf{y}$,
but not be at $\mathbf{y}$.  If all arc-weights are uniform, then this happens at a node $\mathbf{u}$,
see Figure~\ref{fig:bad_example}. 

We now obtain the desired contradiction at $\mathbf{u}$.
Namely, $\beta(\mathbf{u})\supseteq \{r,x\}$, since both these vertices also belong to its ancestor $\mathbf{x}$
and its descendant $\mathbf{z}$.   Since $T(r)\overshadows T(x)$, the cutoff value for this relationship must
therefore be at least than $d(\mathbf{u})$.    On the other hand, $\mathbf{y}$ contains $x$ but not $r$,
which means that the cutoff value must be smaller than $d(\mathbf{y})$, which equals $d(\mathbf{u})$ by choice of $\mathbf{u}$.
Not both are possible.
\end{proof}
\fi


\subsection{Unweighted tree representations?}


Let us end the paper with an open question.    Farber's tree representation
uses a \emph{weighted} host tree, which is rather unusual.   Are the weights
ever necessary, i.e., is there a strongly chordal graph that does not
have a compatible tree representation with uniform arc weights?    

Note that we may assume that all weights are integers by the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:order_treerep},
and it would be easy to replace an edge 
of weight $k>1$ by a path with $k-1$ new subdivision-nodes,    see the dotted nodes
in Figure~\ref{fig:example}.  This maintains a tree
representation if we add the new nodes to trees suitably, but can we always
achieve a compatible tree representation?   

To see that this is non-trivial, consider subdivision-nodes $\mathbf{u_1}$ and $\mathbf{u_2}$
in Figure~\ref{fig:example}.
Clearly they must get added to $T(x)$ and $T(w)$, since these trees include both the
upper and the lower end of the arc that got subdivided by $\mathbf{u_1}$ and $\mathbf{u_2}$.
But should we also add them to $T(y), T(z)$ or $T(a)$?

One can observe that $\mathbf{u_2}$ \emph{must} be added to $T(y)$,
because we have $T(x)\notovershadows T(y)$ due to node $\mathbf{y}$,
so we must maintain that $T(y)\overshadows T(x)$,
and $\mathbf{u_1}\in T(x)$ has the same depth as $\mathbf{c}\in T(x)\cap T(y)$.    
So some of the subtrees that include the upper end must be extended
into the new nodes, even if they do not include the lower end.

On the other hand,
we \emph{must not} add $\mathbf{u_1}$ to $T(z)$,
because we have $T(w)\notovershadows T(z)$ to due to node $\mathbf{z}$, 
so we must maintain that $T(z)\overshadows T(w)$,
and $\mathbf{b}\in T(w)\setminus T(z)$  has the same depth as $\mathbf{u_1}\in T(w)$.
So some of the subtrees that include the upper end \emph{must not} be extended
into the new nodes.

So neither of the two obvious strategies to extend subtrees into new nodes works.
In this specific example one can find a suitable assignment, 
and there is also the option of rearranging the host tree entirely.
%---deleting the top three nodes would maintain a tree
%representation and free up many more ways in which subtrees could be kept
%compatible).    
So it may well be true that all strongly chordal graphs
have compatible tree-representations with unit arc lengths, but this remains open.



